<section
  id='case-littlejoyshop'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Little Joy Shop — Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='React and TypeScript e-commerce case study focused on product discovery, search feedback, cart state, checkout flow, and reusable components.'
  data-case='littlejoyshop'
  aria-labelledby='case-littlejoyshop-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-littlejoyshop-title'>Little Joy Shop</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>A focused React shop experience from product discovery to checkout
          confirmation.</p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual JavaScript Frameworks assignment</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>React, TypeScript, Bootstrap, REST API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Complete e-commerce flow</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='littlejoyshop'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Little Joy Shop sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='littlejoyshop'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The task was to build a small but complete online shop using a supplied product API. Users
        needed to browse and search products, understand individual product details, manage a cart,
        complete a checkout form, and receive a clear confirmation without losing track of their
        choices.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The project looks visually simple, but several UI states depend on the same product and
            cart data. Search results, prices, quantities, totals, and confirmation all needed to
            stay accurate while the user moved between routed views.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Keep product data predictable with TypeScript interfaces</li>
            <li>Maintain clear cart feedback as items and quantities change</li>
            <li>Support empty, loading, error, and no-search-results states</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--products'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-littlejoyshop-600.webp'
            data-src='site-littlejoyshop-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Little Joy Shop product listing showing product cards, prices, and shopping actions'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Responsive product discovery and search</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Used React components for repeated product, navigation, feedback, and page patterns
              while TypeScript made the API and cart data easier to reason about
            </li>
            <li>
              Separated browsing, product details, cart, checkout, and confirmation into routed
              views with a clear step-by-step journey
            </li>
            <li>
              Added an explicit no-results state so an unsuccessful search feels intentional rather
              than like missing or broken content
            </li>
            <li>
              Kept the cart summary visible and readable, with calculated totals and a focused path
              into checkout
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--cart'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-littlejoyshop-cart-600.webp'
            data-src='web-littlejoyshop-cart-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='403'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Little Joy Shop cart showing selected products, quantities, totals, and the checkout action'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Cart review and checkout summary</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The result is a complete, responsive shopping journey built with React and TypeScript. This
        project strengthened my understanding of typed API data, component reuse, routing, and
        state-driven interfaces. It also reinforced that small feedback details—especially empty
        search results and a transparent cart summary—make a straightforward flow feel far more
        reliable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'><use href='#i-arrow-left' /></svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>